From afaa2da2f0f0b5bb2cd20b4db1129df87e17a99b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 01:27:45 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [DVBridge] Low-Latency whose target cannot be used: Standard,
 or none

Issue #25: a projector that takes Dolby Vision Standard, with "Low-Latency
also where the TV takes Standard" on, declares Low-Latency primaries past
BT.2020 (green 0.050 outside it, the rounding allowed is 0.006). Hades
chose Low-Latency for it all the same, refused the target when the first
picture came ("low-latency-mapping"), turned native output off for the
video, and the film played as its HDR10 base layer with its HDR10+
metadata and without its enhancement layer, while Standard with the
enhancement layer worked on the same projector. The log also said
"(the display offers no other)", which was not true there.

The target that Low-Latency maps to is now judged before the signal is
chosen (CWinSystemGbmGLESContext::DVLowLatencyTargetUsable(): the part of
TakeDVLowLatencyMapping() that does not depend on the picture, decided
once a video and Dolby Vision block, its reason logged once), and
DV_DISPLAY::LowLatencyWayOf() decides what Low-Latency comes to:
- asked also where the display takes Standard, and the target cannot be
  used: Standard, which the display takes, with a line in kodi.log;
- the display offers Low-Latency only, and the target cannot be used: no
  native output (the reason "lltarget" on the Dolby Vision page of the
  player information), so that its Dolby Vision plays as on a display
  without it: rebuilt as HDR10 with its enhancement layer where "Show
  Dolby Vision as HDR10 when the display cannot" is on (the default).
The status line says why Low-Latency is sent: the display offers no
other, or the settings ask for it where the display takes Standard.

What counts as a target that can be used does not change here (the
primaries within BT.2020 and the rounding of the block). In the EDID
corpus of Hades, 63 of 224 unique Dolby Vision blocks give a Low-Latency
target past that (60 of them by their blue, all within 0.02); taking such
primaries to BT.2020's edge is a choice of picture left to the user.

The help of the Low-Latency settings, of "Adapt Dolby Vision to my
display (projectors)" and of "Show Dolby Vision as HDR10 when the
display cannot" now says what Standard, Low-Latency and Dolby Vision as
HDR10 are, and what happens where the display's Low-Latency peak and
colours cannot be used (English, and the Spanish of es_es, es_ar and
es_mx). New Estuary string #41173 for the reason.

Host tests: the decision passes 8 of 8 checks, and a mutant without the
target in it (the rule before) fails 3; the blocks of issue #25 and of
the corpus judged by the bridge's own check.
