 #: system/settings/settings.xml
 msgctxt "#39871"
-msgid "Some TV inputs offer Dolby Vision only as Low-Latency (their game mode). With this on, Hades sends Dolby Vision that way and does the tone mapping to your display itself, with its own method and not Dolby's. Off by default: such an input then plays the video as HDR10, and Hades shows a notice. On some TVs, turning off the game mode brings back Dolby Vision Standard. A TV input that takes Standard keeps it. Takes effect from the next video."
+msgid "Dolby Vision comes in two forms. Standard: the TV maps the picture to its screen itself, with Dolby's processing; most Dolby Vision TVs take it, and Hades uses it wherever it can. Low-Latency: the source maps the picture, and the TV shows it as it comes; some TV inputs offer only this form (their game mode). With this on, Hades sends Low-Latency to such an input and does that mapping itself, with its own method and not Dolby's, to the peak and colours the TV gives for it. Off by default: such an input then gets the video as HDR10 (see 'Show Dolby Vision as HDR10 when the display cannot'), and Hades shows a notice. On some TVs, turning off the game mode brings back Standard. Where the peak and colours the TV gives for Low-Latency cannot be used, it gets HDR10 as well; 'Low-Latency: peak and colours set here' sets them by hand. Takes effect from the next video."
 msgstr ""
 
